  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EDecompensation refers to a worsening of heart failure symptoms and signs, often due to congestion or reduced cardiac output. It may develop gradually over days or weeks, so early changes can provide an opportunity to intervene before an emergency presentation.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003ESymptoms to Ask About\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EIncreasing breathlessness on exertion or at rest\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EOrthopnoea or paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ENew or worsening swelling in the legs, ankles or abdomen\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ERapid weight gain, such as more than 2 kg over 2 to 3 days\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EIncreased fatigue, reduced exercise tolerance or poor appetite\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EDizziness, confusion or reduced urine output\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EClinical Assessment\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EAssessment should include heart rate and rhythm, blood pressure, respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, weight and fluid status. Examination may reveal raised jugular venous pressure, lung crackles, a third heart sound, hepatomegaly, ascites or peripheral oedema. Investigations may include electrolytes, renal function, full blood count, troponin where indicated, natriuretic peptides, ECG and chest imaging.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003ECommon Triggers\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECommon triggers include infection, arrhythmia, myocardial ischaemia, uncontrolled hypertension, missed medications, recent medication changes, NSAID use, excess sodium or fluid intake, renal dysfunction, anaemia and thyroid disease.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EWhen to Escalate\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EUrgent assessment is needed for severe breathlessness, chest pain, syncope, new confusion, hypoxia, hypotension, cool peripheries or markedly reduced urine output. Once the patient is stable, clinicians should treat the trigger, review medications, update the action plan and arrange early follow-up.\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
    </bodyContent>
